WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
Marcus and Elena sit down with their household budget.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.440
They want the children to stay in their home and finish

3
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.380
school if either parent dies.

4
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.000
The first step is fact gathering: income, expenses, debts,

5
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.460
dependents, existing coverage, savings, goals and an affordable premium.

6
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.340
Include unpaid work such as childcare and household management.

7
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.420
Losing that support can create a replacement cost even without losing

8
00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.600
a paycheck. The agent should understand who depends on each person

9
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.100
and for how long.

10
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.000
A product name or an income multiplier cannot answer those questions

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.130
by itself. The human-life-value approach looks at the financial value of

12
00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:46.890
future earnings or contributions.

13
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:52.510
A detailed estimate uses expected working years and present-value

14
00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:56.470
assumptions, with appropriate adjustments for amounts not supporting

15
00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:01.070
survivors. The needs approach starts with the specific money the survivors

16
00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:06.850
would require, then subtracts resources available to meet those needs.

17
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.550
These methods can produce different estimates because their assumptions

18
00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.410
differ. Neither number measures a person's worth.

19
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.150
The purpose is to estimate a financial exposure.

20
00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.050
Make the assumptions visible and review them as the household's circumstances

21
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:28.052
change. The family identifies one hundred fifty thousand dollars for a

22
00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:33.272
mortgage payoff, thirty thousand for final expenses and other debts, and

23
00:01:33.272 --> 00:01:34.950
forty thousand for education.

24
00:01:35.852 --> 00:01:39.592
Those stated lump-sum needs total two hundred twenty thousand dollars.

25
00:01:40.332 --> 00:01:44.512
They also estimate an ongoing income shortfall of three thousand dollars

26
00:01:44.512 --> 00:01:46.332
per month for ten years.

27
00:01:47.092 --> 00:01:52.212
In a deliberately simplified calculation with no interest or inflation, that

28
00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:54.892
income need is three hundred sixty thousand dollars.

29
00:01:55.412 --> 00:01:56.952
Keep the categories separate.

30
00:01:58.052 --> 00:02:01.232
If the mortgage is paid off, do not also include the

31
00:02:01.232 --> 00:02:03.692
same mortgage payments in the future monthly shortfall.

32
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:08.490
The simplified needs total is five hundred eighty thousand dollars.

33
00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:12.850
The family identifies eighty thousand dollars of savings actually available

34
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:17.050
for these needs and one hundred thousand dollars of existing death

35
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.550
protection assumed to remain in force.

36
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.930
Subtracting both leaves a four hundred thousand dollar gap.

37
00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.630
This is a teaching example, not a quote or a recommendation.

38
00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.840
Verify whether employer coverage continues, whether assets have

39
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.900
other commitments and whether a survivor can realistically earn the assumed

40
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.770
income. A resource should not be counted twice or treated as

41
00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.950
immediately available when access is restricted.

42
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.320
A family may own a valuable home or business but have

43
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.420
little ready cash. Liquidity means money is available

44
00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.080
to meet obligations without a forced sale.

45
00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.960
A life benefit may help with final expenses, debts or applicable

46
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.160
estate costs and help preserve assets for survivors.

47
00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:10.240
Estate planning also considers the owner, beneficiary and legal arrangement;

48
00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.060
it is broader than simply buying a policy.

49
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.920
Some permanent policies offer cash accumulation during life,

50
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.460
subject to their contract, charges and funding.

51
00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.240
Keep that feature distinct from the death benefit and from money

52
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.740
guaranteed to be immediately accessible.

53
00:03:33.858 --> 00:03:38.658
Social Security survivor benefits may help eligible spouses, children or

54
00:03:38.658 --> 00:03:41.458
dependent parents based on the deceased worker's record.

55
00:03:42.258 --> 00:03:45.338
They do not automatically replace the household's full income.

56
00:03:46.238 --> 00:03:51.398
Eligibility, ages, work history, earnings and family maximums can affect

57
00:03:51.398 --> 00:03:56.398
payment. A child's benefit and a surviving spouse's caregiving benefit can

58
00:03:56.398 --> 00:03:59.438
end at different times, creating an income gap.

59
00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:04.078
Use an actual benefit estimate and the expected payment period rather

60
00:04:04.078 --> 00:04:05.318
than assuming an amount.

61
00:04:06.118 --> 00:04:11.078
Then review the full plan: immediate needs, ongoing support, available

62
00:04:11.078 --> 00:04:16.338
resources, affordable coverage and the changes
that should trigger a new review.
